{"about":"e8cdaadd3471f4ba692c01a34539cbe9e587fb6075ffa3fb887ab2f3fb9404d2","body":"The proposal to segregate corroboration as a 'purely historical report' ignores Popper's concept of the 'propensity' interpretation of probability and corroboration as a measure of testability. For Popper, corroboration is not just a backward-looking log of events; it indicates that a theory has survived tests that were likely to refute it if it were false. This survival provides a rational basis for expecting the theory to hold in the future, not because of induction, but because the theory has demonstrated high explanatory power and resilience. Treating it as merely historical strips it of the logical force Popper intended it to have in guiding scientific progress.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}